<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.9.2: Writing an Equation to Represent a Constraint</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00205</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.9.2: Writing an Equation to Represent a Constraint</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7358a2ee-0135-443f-96a4-7602b7493d38</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>An automobile manufacturer is preparing a shipment of cars and trucks on a cargo ship that can carry 21,600 tons.</para>
<para>The cars weigh 3.6 tons each, and the trucks weigh 7.5 tons each.</para>
<figure><media alt="A ship carrying cargo containers."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/52944e1be71fe641b162040c90b75bcfcb0b3105"/></media></figure><!--Q1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation that represents the weight constraint of a shipment. Let \( c \) be the number of cars and \( t
        \) be the number of trucks. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter you answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( 3.6c+7.5t=21,600 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For numbers 2 - 5, use the following situation:
  For one shipment, trucks are loaded first and cars are loaded afterward. (Even though trucks are bulkier than cars, a shipment can consist of all trucks as long as it is within the weight limit.)
</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="916849c7-76cb-4449-a676-f8c36ffb4e57" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="cdfd9a5c-09c6-496b-8513-a9644a629f57" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="5000">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has 480 trucks.</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 5000</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="c7a64605-1755-4589-a66b-cbafa3668d84" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2875">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has 1500 trucks.</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 2875</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9242f6f9-b898-46bf-8492-91431dba28a3" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="300">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has 2736 trucks.</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 300</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>

<!--Q5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has \(t\) trucks</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac{21,600-7.5t}{3.6}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para> Use the following information for 6 - 8. For a different shipment, cars are loaded first, and then trucks are loaded afterward.</para>

<!--Q6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation you could enter into a calculator or a spreadsheet tool to find the number of trucks that can be shipped if the number of cars is known.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \( t=\dfrac{21,\!600-3.6c}{7.5} \), where \( c \) is the number of cars.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="1c47c912-8e3b-4f49-9589-d8f3218a8e3a" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ddd2a6db-692e-4c7e-892d-9596a9d59ab0" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2400">
    <problem>
      <para>Use your equation and a calculator or a computer to find the number of trucks that can be shipped if the cargo already has 1000 cars.</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 2400</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0eefd012-aa54-4abc-b55b-09f5e29a762a" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="840">
    <problem>
      <para>Use your equation and a calculator or a computer to find the number of trucks that can be shipped if the cargo already has 4250 cars.</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 840</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
